WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.000
Welcome. This class replaces impressive
agent demos with repeatable release

2
00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:14.000
evidence. You will define testable
behavior claims, build representative and

3
00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:21.000
adversarial cases, score outcomes and
trajectories, compare a candidate with

4
00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:28.000
a baseline, and turn production failures
into governed regression tests.

5
00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:33.667
An evaluation is a repeatable experiment,
not a guided demonstration.

6
00:00:33.667 --> 00:00:39.333
Start with a claim that evidence can
confirm or reject.

7
00:00:39.333 --> 00:00:45.000
Identify the workflow and configuration
version, target population, expected

8
00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:50.667
outcome, unacceptable behavior, scoring
rules, sample, environment, budgets, and

9
00:00:50.667 --> 00:00:56.333
release threshold before running the
candidate. Separate task success from

10
00:00:56.333 --> 00:01:02.000
style, safety, policy compliance,
efficiency, and user experience. A

11
00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:07.667
pleasant answer may violate authority. A
safe trajectory may still

12
00:01:07.667 --> 00:01:13.333
produce an unusable result. One average
cannot express both. Give

13
00:01:13.333 --> 00:01:19.000
every case a stable identifier, slice,
versioned input fixture, observable

14
00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:24.667
expected outcome, allowed tool trajectory,
forbidden behavior, rubric, and latency,

15
00:01:24.667 --> 00:01:30.333
cost, turn, or retry budget. Predeclared
claims and thresholds reduce

16
00:01:30.333 --> 00:01:36.000
the temptation to redefine success after
seeing results.

17
00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:41.077
Build a set that reflects the product, not
merely what

18
00:01:41.077 --> 00:01:46.154
is easy to pass. Include common tasks,
important user journeys,

19
00:01:46.154 --> 00:01:51.231
supported languages and modalities,
high-impact groups, difficult long-tail

20
00:01:51.231 --> 00:01:56.308
cases, and actual data distributions.
Preserve a holdout set so

21
00:01:56.308 --> 00:02:01.385
repeated tuning does not overfit every
known example. Add boundaries

22
00:02:01.385 --> 00:02:06.462
and adversarial conditions: ambiguous
intent, prompt injection, missing

23
00:02:06.462 --> 00:02:11.538
permission, stale or conflicting evidence,
malformed tool output, timeouts, unsafe

24
00:02:11.538 --> 00:02:16.615
requests, and policy conflicts. Every
confirmed production incident should yield

25
00:02:16.615 --> 00:02:21.692
the smallest reproducing case after
privacy review. Synthetic generation can

26
00:02:21.692 --> 00:02:26.769
broaden coverage, but a human must decide
whether each synthetic

27
00:02:26.769 --> 00:02:31.846
case represents a real product behavior or
risk. Track source,

28
00:02:31.846 --> 00:02:36.923
consent, transformation, retention, and
slice membership so a case remains

29
00:02:36.923 --> 00:02:42.000
governed and interpretable.

30
00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:46.857
Score both what happened and how it
happened. Deterministic graders

31
00:02:46.857 --> 00:02:51.714
fit schemas, exact constraints,
calculations, permission decisions, tool

32
00:02:51.714 --> 00:02:56.571
arguments, postconditions, terminal
states, and latency or cost budgets. Human

33
00:02:56.571 --> 00:03:01.429
reviewers fit nuanced usefulness, clarity,
and context-dependent judgment when guided

34
00:03:01.429 --> 00:03:06.286
by an explicit rubric and blinded to
candidate identity. Model

35
00:03:06.286 --> 00:03:11.143
graders can add scalable signals, but they
are not ground

36
00:03:11.143 --> 00:03:16.000
truth. Calibrate them against
human-labeled examples, version their

37
00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:20.857
prompts and models, measure agreement by
slice, and route material

38
00:03:20.857 --> 00:03:25.714
disagreement for review. For agent runs,
inspect tool choice, input

39
00:03:25.714 --> 00:03:30.571
validation, approvals, authority, retry
count, evidence preservation, handoffs,

40
00:03:30.571 --> 00:03:35.429
verification, and final state. A correct
final sentence can hide

41
00:03:35.429 --> 00:03:40.286
a forbidden tool call or duplicate write.
An imperfect but

42
00:03:40.286 --> 00:03:45.143
safely contained run may reveal a
recoverable product defect instead

43
00:03:45.143 --> 00:03:50.000
of a safety failure.

44
00:03:50.000 --> 00:03:55.200
Consider an agent that creates a correct
support summary. The

45
00:03:55.200 --> 00:04:00.400
final-answer grader awards full points.
The trajectory check finds that

46
00:04:00.400 --> 00:04:05.600
the agent queried an unrelated customer
record, skipped approval, and

47
00:04:05.600 --> 00:04:10.800
retried a write after an unknown timeout.
Permission and postcondition

48
00:04:10.800 --> 00:04:16.000
checks fail, so the case is a critical
failure despite

49
00:04:16.000 --> 00:04:21.200
the polished summary. A second run asks
for clarification, stays

50
00:04:21.200 --> 00:04:26.400
within one account, records approval, and
stops after a provider

51
00:04:26.400 --> 00:04:31.600
error. It earns fewer usefulness points
but passes safety and

52
00:04:31.600 --> 00:04:36.800
recovery criteria. This comparison shows
why one overall impression is

53
00:04:36.800 --> 00:04:42.000
not a release gate.

54
00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:47.231
Compare baseline and candidate on
identical case versions, fixtures,

55
00:04:47.231 --> 00:04:52.462
environment, budgets, grader definitions,
and sampling rules. Report pass rate

56
00:04:52.462 --> 00:04:57.692
and score distribution by slice, not only
a global mean.

57
00:04:57.692 --> 00:05:02.923
Include critical failures, grader
disagreement, latency percentiles, cost

58
00:05:02.923 --> 00:05:08.154
per successful task, retries, and
uncertainty. Before the run, declare

59
00:05:08.154 --> 00:05:13.385
must-not-regress slices and the minimum
practical improvement that justifies

60
00:05:13.385 --> 00:05:18.615
change. A higher average cannot excuse a
new permission bypass,

61
00:05:18.615 --> 00:05:23.846
cross-tenant disclosure, uncontrolled
purchase, or unreconciled write.

62
00:05:23.846 --> 00:05:29.077
Investigate changed cases individually and
record whether the difference comes

63
00:05:29.077 --> 00:05:34.308
from model behavior, prompt, tool,
retrieval, policy, orchestration, or

64
00:05:34.308 --> 00:05:39.538
infrastructure. The release decision is
ship, hold, or rollback with

65
00:05:39.538 --> 00:05:44.769
named evidence, reviewer, residual risk,
and follow-up work. Safety, authority,

66
00:05:44.769 --> 00:05:50.000
and high-impact behavior require
accountable human sign-off.

67
00:05:50.000 --> 00:05:55.818
Make evaluation a controlled learning
loop. Privacy-reviewed production traces,

68
00:05:55.818 --> 00:06:01.636
explicit feedback, escalations, and
incidents can reveal cases the offline

69
00:06:01.636 --> 00:06:07.455
set missed. Remove or transform sensitive
content while preserving the

70
00:06:07.455 --> 00:06:13.273
behavior that caused failure. Apply
consent, access, retention, deletion, and

71
00:06:13.273 --> 00:06:19.091
provenance requirements before adding
anything to the corpus. Version the

72
00:06:19.091 --> 00:06:24.909
case, fixture, expected behavior, rubric,
deterministic checks, model-grader

73
00:06:24.909 --> 00:06:30.727
configuration, workflow, tools, policies,
environment, and results. Keep prior

74
00:06:30.727 --> 00:06:36.545
versions so release and rollback decisions
remain reproducible. Monitor case

75
00:06:36.545 --> 00:06:42.364
duplication, slice balance, label quality,
grader drift, and holdout contamination.

76
00:06:42.364 --> 00:06:48.182
A dashboard score without the inputs and
decision rules that

77
00:06:48.182 --> 00:06:54.000
produced it is an observation, not durable
release evidence.

78
00:06:54.000 --> 00:07:00.667
Choose one agent workflow. Write the
release claim, one must-not-regress

79
00:07:00.667 --> 00:07:07.333
slice, its observable threshold, and one
trajectory failure that a

80
00:07:07.333 --> 00:07:14.000
final-answer score would miss.

81
00:08:44.000 --> 00:08:50.000
Checkpoint. The candidate improves the
average score but fails one

82
00:08:50.000 --> 00:08:56.000
critical permission case. May the team
ship because the aggregate

83
00:08:56.000 --> 00:09:02.000
improved?

84
00:10:02.000 --> 00:10:07.250
Do not ship on the average. Honor the
must-not-regress permission

85
00:10:07.250 --> 00:10:12.500
gate, inspect the failed case, and record
a hold or

86
00:10:12.500 --> 00:10:17.750
rollback until the violation is corrected
and retested. If you

87
00:10:17.750 --> 00:10:23.000
chose to adjust the case weight after the
result, return

88
00:10:23.000 --> 00:10:28.250
to the predeclared threshold; post-hoc
weighting makes the decision

89
00:10:28.250 --> 00:10:33.500
untrustworthy. If you rejected every
candidate regression, refine the rule:

90
00:10:33.500 --> 00:10:38.750
noncritical tradeoffs may be accepted when
thresholds, uncertainty, reviewer

91
00:10:38.750 --> 00:10:44.000
approval, and residual risk are explicit.

92
00:10:44.000 --> 00:10:50.800
Open the agent evaluation activity. Build
ten versioned cases, a

93
00:10:50.800 --> 00:10:57.600
100-point rubric, and deterministic checks
for schema, permissions, postconditions,

94
00:10:57.600 --> 00:11:04.400
turns, latency, and cost. Blind-score
baseline and candidate, review

95
00:11:04.400 --> 00:11:11.200
disagreement by slice, choose ship, hold,
or rollback using predeclared

96
00:11:11.200 --> 00:11:18.000
thresholds, and add one failure to the
regression set.

97
00:14:19.000 --> 00:14:26.000
When ready, begin the knowledge check. You
will place thresholds

98
00:14:26.000 --> 00:14:33.000
before results, score agent trajectories,
calibrate model graders, enforce critical

99
00:14:33.000 --> 00:14:40.000
slices, and identify the versions required
for reproducibility.

100
00:14:40.000 --> 00:14:44.667
Predeclare the claim, cover reality and
failure, score outcome and

101
00:14:44.667 --> 00:14:49.333
trajectory, compare identical cases, honor
critical gates, and preserve every

102
00:14:49.333 --> 00:14:54.000
